# Bott periodicity, the eightfold rhythm running through the real Clifford algebras and the topology of the division
# algebras. A real Clifford algebra on n generators has an algebra type, a pattern of real, complex and quaternionic
# matrix blocks, and as n grows that type repeats with period EIGHT: the algebra on n plus eight generators is the algebra
# on n tensored with the sixteen-by-sixteen real matrices. So the same eight that is the dimension of the octonions is the
# period of Clifford algebra and of the stable homotopy of the orthogonal group. This file MODELS the eight Clifford
# classes as a cycle and DEFINES adding a generator as the step around it. It PROVES the period is eight, adding eight
# generators returns to the same class for every class, and that it is not four, four steps land on the opposite class, so
# eight is the true period. The sixteen-by-sixteen block that realizes one period, two hundred fifty-six, is recorded as a
# machine-integer count. The period theorem is quantified over the actual eight-element type and proved by induction.

# The eight Clifford algebra classes, the period of the type as generators are added.
form clifford-class
  case class-zero
  case class-one
  case class-two
  case class-three
  case class-four
  case class-five
  case class-six
  case class-seven

# Adding one generator advances the Clifford class by one, wrapping around after eight, the Bott period.
task add-generator
  take c, like clifford-class
  like clifford-class
  fork case, read c
    case class-zero
      send back
        make class-one
    case class-one
      send back
        make class-two
    case class-two
      send back
        make class-three
    case class-three
      send back
        make class-four
    case class-four
      send back
        make class-five
    case class-five
      send back
        make class-six
    case class-six
      send back
        make class-seven
    case class-seven
      send back
        make class-zero

# BOTT PERIODICITY: adding eight generators returns to the same Clifford class, add-generator applied eight times is the
# identity, for every class. So the type of the real Clifford algebras has period eight. Proved by induction over the
# eight classes.
rule adding-eight-generators-returns-to-the-same-class
  seat c, like clifford-class
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-generator
        call add-generator
          call add-generator
            call add-generator
              call add-generator
                call add-generator
                  call add-generator
                    call add-generator
                      read c
      read c
  fold c

# THE PERIOD IS NOT FOUR: adding four generators to class zero lands on class four, the opposite class, not back on class
# zero, so eight is the true period and not a smaller one. This is the real-to-quaternionic swap at the halfway point.
rule adding-four-generators-lands-opposite
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add-generator
        call add-generator
          call add-generator
            call add-generator
              make class-zero
      make class-four
  calm hold

# THE PERIOD BLOCK as a machine-integer count: the Clifford algebra on eight generators is the sixteen-by-sixteen real
# matrix algebra, 16 * 16 = 256, the block that tensors on to realize one full period.
hold clifford-eight-is-sixteen-by-sixteen
  call is-equal
    code 256
    call multiply
      code 16
      code 16

# THE PARALLELIZABLE SPHERES sit one below the division-algebra dimensions, the same eightfold ceiling: the seven-sphere
# is parallelizable because 8 = 7 + 1, the octonion case, the largest such sphere.
hold seven-sphere-is-one-below-eight
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call add
      code 7
      code 1
